---
import { RiArrowLeftLine } from "@remixicon/react"

import { Badge } from "@/components/ui/badge"
import { buttonVariants } from "@/components/ui/button"
import MethodologyFaq from "@/components/methodology-faq"
import { Separator } from "@/components/ui/separator"
import Layout from "@/layouts/main.astro"
import { loadRankings } from "@/lib/data"
import { formatDate } from "@/lib/format"

const { meta } = loadRankings()

const queryExample = `language:JavaScript fork:false mirror:false stars:>=100 created:${meta.window.from}..${meta.window.to}`
const apiUrl = `https://api.github.com/search/repositories?q=${encodeURIComponent(queryExample)}&per_page=1`

const sectionHeadingClass =
  "flex items-baseline gap-4 font-heading text-lg font-semibold tracking-widest uppercase md:text-xl"
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL
---

<Layout
  title="Methodology — lang.trending"
  description="How lang.trending measures programming language popularity: GitHub search queries, a rolling one-year window, and a throttled pipeline that authenticates in CI via the auto-injected GITHUB_TOKEN."
>
  <section class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-6 pt-16 pb-24 md:pt-20">
    <a href={base} class={buttonVariants({ variant: "ghost", size: "sm" })}>
      <RiArrowLeftLine data-icon="inline-start" />
      Back to the ranking
    </a>

    <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <Badge variant="secondary">
        Window: {formatDate(meta.window.from)} → {formatDate(meta.window.to)}
      </Badge>
      <Badge variant="secondary">{meta.languagesTracked} languages</Badge>
      <Badge variant="secondary">3 star tiers</Badge>
      <Badge variant="secondary">Updated daily</Badge>
    </div>

    <h1 class="mt-8 text-4xl leading-[1.02] font-medium tracking-tight text-balance md:text-6xl">
      How the <em class="text-viz-accent italic">numbers</em> are gathered.
    </h1>

    <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground md:text-lg">
      Every number on this site is a single count from the public GitHub search
      API: the number of repositories created in a rolling one-year window,
      tagged with a given language. There is no sampling, no estimation, and no
      secret sauce — just a deliberately slow, carefully scoped pipeline.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-16 space-y-16">
      <div>
        <h2 class={sectionHeadingClass}>
          <span class="text-viz-accent tabular-nums">01</span> What counts
        </h2>
        <ul class="mt-5 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          <li>
            A repository counts if it was <em>created</em> inside the window and
            is tagged with the language.
          </li>
          <li>
            The base tier requires at least 2 stars — zero- and one-star
            repositories (dominated by throwaway experiments and static-site
            detritus) are excluded from every count.
          </li>
          <li>Forks and mirrors are excluded from every query.</li>
          <li>
            Only public repositories are visible to the search API — for
            everyone, with or without a token.
          </li>
          <li>
            The count is the search result's{" "}
            <code class="font-serif">total_count</code> — GitHub computes it, we
            don't.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class={sectionHeadingClass}>
          <span class="text-viz-accent tabular-nums">02</span> The window
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          The "last year" is never hardcoded. On every build, the window is
          computed as <em>today minus 365 days → today</em>, in UTC, inclusive
          on both ends.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class={sectionHeadingClass}>
          <span class="text-viz-accent tabular-nums">03</span> The query
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Three queries per language — one per star tier. Here is the exact
          query behind the JavaScript count in the top tier:
        </p>
        <pre class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto border border-border bg-muted/40 p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code>{queryExample}</code></pre>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          The other tiers are the same query with{" "}
          <code class="font-serif">stars:&gt;=20</code> or{" "}
          <code class="font-serif">stars:&gt;=2</code> — every threshold is
          inclusive, and repositories below 2 stars are deliberately out of
          scope. Served from{" "}
          <code class="break-all font-serif text-sm">api.github.com</code> with{" "}
          <code class="font-serif">per_page=1</code> — only the total matters,
          never the results.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class={sectionHeadingClass}>
          <span class="text-viz-accent tabular-nums">04</span> Rate limits &amp;
          throttling
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Local builds call the API without credentials and are bound by the
          public search limit:{" "}
          <strong class="text-foreground">10 requests per minute</strong> per IP
          address — and GitHub Actions shares runner IPs across many
          repositories, so that pool can be exhausted by strangers. The daily CI
          build therefore authenticates with the automatically injected{" "}
          <code class="font-serif">GITHUB_TOKEN</code>: a dedicated{" "}
          <strong class="text-foreground">30 requests per minute</strong> per
          build. The fetcher paces itself to exactly one request every 6 seconds
          (unauthenticated) or 2 seconds (authenticated), honors{" "}
          <code class="font-serif">x-ratelimit-remaining</code> and{" "}
          <code class="font-serif">retry-after</code> headers, and waits out the
          documented reset window when the budget runs dry. A full refresh —
          three tiers across {meta.languagesTracked} languages,{" "}
          {meta.languagesTracked * 3} requests — takes under five minutes in CI
          and under twelve minutes locally.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class={sectionHeadingClass}>
          <span class="text-viz-accent tabular-nums">05</span> Scope —
          mainstream only
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          This is a popularity index for the languages people actually build
          with, not a census of every language GitHub can tag. The catalog is
          hand-curated to common, general-purpose languages and the widely used
          special-purpose ones (web markup, data science, systems scripting).
          Esoteric and niche languages are intentionally excluded, and the
          catalog is the same every day so comparisons stay apples-to-apples.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class={sectionHeadingClass}>
          <span class="text-viz-accent tabular-nums">06</span> Caveats
        </h2>
        <ul class="mt-5 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          <li>
            A language tag is the repository's <em>primary</em> language from
            GitHub's Linguist detection, and it can occasionally mis-tag a
            repository. A repository that mixes several languages only counts
            toward its primary one — crediting every language in every
            repository would take one API call per repository, which no rate
            limit could sustain.
          </li>
          <li>
            Counts can drift slightly between builds as the search index
            settles.
          </li>
          <li>
            A repository may appear in more than one language's count if its tag
            changed mid-window.
          </li>
          <li>
            Star tiers are counted at fetch time — a repository that gains its
            100th star a day later is counted in the tier it was in on fetch
            day.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class={sectionHeadingClass}>
          <span class="text-viz-accent tabular-nums">07</span> The weighted
          score
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          The weighted ranking in the Field notes folds the three tiers into one
          composite. Each tier's count is multiplied by a weight that grows with
          the threshold — <strong class="text-foreground">1×</strong> at ≥2
          stars, <strong class="text-foreground">2×</strong> at ≥20,{" "}
          <strong class="text-foreground">4×</strong> at ≥100 — and summed per
          language. Because the tiers are nested, a repository that cleared
          every threshold contributes 1+2+4 = 7 points while one below 2 stars
          contributes nothing at all: the harder a repository was to earn, the
          more it counts. It is pure arithmetic on the same{" "}
          {meta.languagesTracked * 3} counts — no extra API requests.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Separator className="my-16" />

    <h2 class={sectionHeadingClass}>
      <span class="text-viz-accent tabular-nums">FAQ</span> Frequently asked
    </h2>
    <MethodologyFaq client:load />

    <div class="mt-16 border border-border p-8">
      <p class="font-serif text-2xl leading-snug md:text-3xl">
        Curious about a specific count?{" "}
        <a
          class="text-viz-accent underline decoration-1 underline-offset-4 transition-opacity hover:opacity-80"
          href={apiUrl}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Run the exact query yourself
        </a>{" "}
        — it's public.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
